# Build context — deny everything, then name what the Dockerfile copies.
#
# This was a list of things to leave out, and the list was wrong in a way that
# is hard to see: `.env` in a .dockerignore is matched against the context root,
# so it excluded a top-level `.env` that does not exist and did nothing about
# `platform/.env`, which holds the runner registration token and the Grafana
# password. That file was sent to the daemon on every build. It never reached
# an image — the Dockerfile copies three things and that is not one of them —
# so this is about blast radius rather than a leak that happened. F5 in
# docs/security-review-2026-08-10.md.
#
# Inverted rather than patched, because `**/.env` would have fixed this
# instance and left the shape of the mistake in place. A deny-list is only ever
# as good as someone's memory of what exists; the next secret to land in this
# repository would have been outside it too. An allow-list cannot be
# out-of-date in that direction: a new file is excluded until someone says
# otherwise, and the only way to get it wrong is to forget something the build
# needs — which fails loudly at COPY, in CI, on the first run.
#
# The three entries below are exactly the Dockerfile's COPY sources. If you add
# a COPY, add it here in the same commit or the build will tell you.

*

!app/
!requirements.txt
!gunicorn.conf.py

# Re-excluded from inside app/, which the line above re-included wholesale.
# Compiled bytecode from a host interpreter is at best dead weight in the image
# and at worst a stale .pyc shadowing a source file that was edited.
app/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
